  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost and/or freezing temperatures this \nweekend.\n\nMid level trough axis is crossing the Appalachians as of mid\nmorning. Anafrontal rain continues in southern portions of the\nforecast area but should depart by this afternoon. Drier air in\nthe wake of the upper jet streak is seen in water vapor imagery\nand is leading to cloud breaks west of the Blue Ridge. Gradual\nclearing should spread eastward through this afternoon.\n\nTemperatures drop today in the wake of a strong cold front with\nhighs expected to be in the 50s for most (40s mtns). Gusty winds\nof 25-35 mph continue through the day before decreasing tonight.\nTonight's cold air will be driven mainly by advection, although\nsome decoupling may occur west of the Blue Ridge which will be\ncloser to the surface high. Winds remain much more uncertain to\nthe east, and while it will be chilly, this may maintain notable\ntemperature/dew point spreads (the latter of which will be in\nthe upper 20s) to prevent frost formation. Unless 12Z guidance \nshifts, further expansion of the Frost Advisory is unlikely. \nWhile the Freeze Warning area still appears to be in good shape,\nit may not be a widespread freeze along/east of I-81.\n\nTemperatures begin gradually warming on Sunday although\nadditional Frost Advisories are possible Sunday night west of\nthe Blue Ridge where low temperatures drop into the 30s (in any\nareas where the growing season does not end tonight). Beginning\nearly next week, chances for Frost/freeze diminish as \ntemperatures continue to warm. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily chances for showers next week as a Nor'easter \ndevelops off the Carolina coast.\n\nAs an upper trough advances through the Mid-Atlantic today, part of \nit cuts off along a stalled front south of our region this weekend. \nHeading into next week, this cutoff upper-level low results in the \ndevelopment of a nor'easter off the Carolina coast. This system then \nmeanders offshore and near the coast, but guidance is uncertain on \nits track and strength. Forecast models show considerable spread \nwith respect to the location nor'easter next week. Some solutions \nkeep the system offshore, while others have it retrograding further \nwestward. If this system does track further westward, it could lead \nto local impacts. With this mid-level low being cut off from the \nmain steering flow, it could linger through much of next week. \nNortheast breezes and daily chances for showers are possible. \nRegions along and east of I-95 have the best potential for showers \nnext week.",
